ose range  first off WTF but let's continue then  shank says that he admits they can still  give quite a scare so we have a  dangerous and experienced pirate who  loses an arm like it was nothing saying  that a court gun can be scary this is  followed by his crew member pulling the  trigger and the man falls like in the  original in this version however when we  get a shot of shanks of serious  expression he's heard snickering and as  the pirate falls down he says when he  wakes up  someone tell him it was a cat gun so  that serious moment that intense silence  that was imbued with so much meaning in  gravity is totally offset instead of a  death we got a guy fainting from a cap  gun and then just when you thought it  couldn't get any worse a guy says it was  alright home oh man that is bad  this scene really illustrates how much  is lost in the for kids translation how  mature themes and moments become bad  jokes as a result the four kids aversion  seems less like a faithful translation  and more like a parody of the original  stop whining landlubbers you're not  dealing with Saints here alright I think  we spent enough time on this scene  moving on number eight minimising  violence another thing that four kids  did regularly was edit violent scenes  that they felt were too graphic or  problematic in some other way we can see  this throughout the show for example  when Zorro gets bad by buggy or when  Luffy uses his sweat to fight crocodile  rather than his blood however one of the  most interesting and ridiculous examples  of censorship can be found in episode 1  when Coby starts freaking out and  telling Luffy that there is no way he  can be the Pirate King Luffy not only  sees Colby acting like a wimp but he has  to listen to someone  tell him that his life's ambition data  breach in one piece and becoming the  Pirate King is impossible Luffy can't  take it anymore and punch his Kobe in  the head when Kobe asks why he did it  Luffy says just because however through  this act of violence we can see just how  much Lucy's dream means to him although  he seemed like a pretty carefree guy up  to this point and remember this is the  first episode so we're still getting to  know what kind of character our  protagonist is this act of violence  shows Luffy's determination and how he  won't let anyone get in the way of his  dream it shows us just how much he does  care when it comes to becoming the  Pirate King this is a great instance of  characterization but it's completely  lost in the for kids version in this  version they edit away roofies hand  Coby thinks that Luffy is going to hit  him so he ducks and this Luffy clone  says I wasn't going to hit you oh so  what happened is that this character  does the exact opposite of what the  original loopy does so the question at  this point is can we even consider these  guys to be the same person at what point  is Luffy no longer Luffy this is just  one example but it's emblematic of a  larger problem through censorship or can  slowly but surely changes these  characters to the point where they're  almost unrecognizable and believe it or  not it gets worse  number 9 no deaths the decision to not  include important deaths not only alters  the tone of the show it also creates  unresolved issues for instance Bell  Amir's death is one of the most  emotionally charged moments in one piece  no lie  I just rewatched a section of the scene  for the video and my eyes are literally  watering and goosebumps are in overdrive  how Bella Mia cannot say that she  doesn't have a family even if it cost  her her life that last I love you before  she dies the death is a sad as Bella  means love for her adopted daughters is  beautiful not to mention that such a  charged moment plays a major role in  nominees characterization and the fact  that she joins the Arlen pirates after  that takes on even greater significance  however this one of the saddest and most  beautiful scenes is all lost in the for  kids